recommended_test:
Implement and judge this as a data-definition fix, like D-19 and D-24, not on two-year D2.

The change: in sql/09_build_lines.sql (line_claims), behind a new config switch `c12_open_recovery`, set
net_cost = incurred_to_date - recoveries - CASE WHEN switch AND line='motor' AND status='open' THEN incurred_to_date * share(peril) ELSE 0 END.
share(peril) = sum(recoveries)/sum(incurred_to_date) over closed motor claims of the same peril with loss date up to the valuation date. That gives about 0.236 for own damage and 0.040 for theft, and 0 for every other peril and line. Putting the change in sql/09 rather than sql/10 means the sql/10 report-lag pattern and quarter factors are rebuilt on the corrected net cost.

Unit test (tests/):
- closed own-damage claims with recoveries have a ratio of 0.8 (±0.001) and theft 0.3;
- no open claim has recoveries above 0;
- no home line has any recoveries;
- the switch off reproduces the current line_claims exactly.

Run run_glm_lines.py with the switch off, then on (the owner's normal runner; I did not run it). Expected results:
- 2023 backtest motor D2 unchanged to 4dp (0.1010);
- 2024 developed motor down by 1.46m ±0.05m (-1.24%);
- 2024 backtest D2 down by about 0.0003 against the new target, from the basis change and not a regression;
- 2025 motor mean prediction down about 1.2-1.4%, and the motor trend slope down about 0.4 points a year.

Record it as a definitional decision. If C1 or #151 applies a motor level factor, measure that factor on the adjusted 2024 target so the two are not double counted. Do not judge it on the leaderboard.

Scripts that reproduce my numbers (run from the pack root with .venv/bin/python): <review scratch>/agents/verify-C12/c12_measure.py and <review scratch>/agents/verify-C12/c12_trend.py.
